{"name":"Electric Arc","hex":"#f7f7cb","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electric arc","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A discharge of electricity across a gap, causing ionization of the air (or other gas) and resulting in a bright, hot plasma."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electric_arc"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electric arc","blocks":[{"text":"An electric arc is an electrical breakdown of a gas that produces a prolonged electrical discharge. The current through a normally nonconductive medium such as air produces a plasma, which may produce visible light. An arc discharge is initiated either by thermionic emission or by field emission. After initiation, the arc relies on thermionic emission of electrons from the electrodes supporting the arc.\nAn arc discharge is characterized by a lower voltage than a glow discharge. An archaic term is voltaic arc, as used in the phrase \"voltaic arc lamp\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg/330px-Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_arc","wikidata":"Q207456"},{"title":"Electricity","blocks":[{"text":"Electricity is the set of physical phenomena associated with the presence and motion of matter possessing an electric charge. Electricity is related to magnetism, both being part of the phenomenon of electromagnetism, as described by Maxwell's equations. Common phenomena are related to electricity, including lightning, static electricity, electric heating, electric discharges and many others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg/330px-London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity","wikidata":"Q12725"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg/500px-Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg/500px-Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electric arc between two nails","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Lightning_NOAA.jpg/500px-Lightning_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Lightning_NOAA.jpg/1280px-Lightning_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Lightning_NOAA.jpg/500px-Lightning_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Natural lightning is an electric spark because it is a brief pulse, not an arc (which is continuous)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALightning_NOAA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Arcing_pickup_shoe.jpg/500px-Arcing_pickup_shoe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Arcing_pickup_shoe.jpg/500px-Arcing_pickup_shoe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Electricity arcs between the power rail and electrical pickup \"shoe\" on a London Underground train","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArcing_pickup_shoe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Electric_arc.jpg/500px-Electric_arc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Electric_arc.jpg/128